# Codec implementation and shipping

Activate this workflow when asked to implement, build, finish, harden, or ship an audio, video, or image codec decoder, encoder, or both, or to complete a codec implementation already in progress. Do not apply it to networking codecs, text encodings, or unrelated serialization.

Before changing codec code, read `.cursor/skills/ship-codec/SKILL.md` and follow its applicable references. Resolve the requested codec and direction from the request and active task. Preserve decoder-only or encoder-only scope; “both” requires independently validated encode and decode paths.

Read all project rules under `.cursor/rules`, their scope and referenced instructions, applicable `AGENTS.md`, manifests, dependency policy, and relevant CI. Respect the instructions governing each path; this rule supplements existing architecture/style requirements. Report missing instructions accurately and proceed with available requirements when safe.

For Cutlass, preserve native safe Rust, one codec crate per codec, inherited lints, dependency policy, and separate codec/container/platform boundaries. Do not substitute another complete codec, foreign library/binary, subprocess, or platform implementation for the requested native software codec. Test oracles are isolated, project-permitted validation tools.

Before implementing, record an explicit profile/tool/configuration support matrix, unsupported cases, resource limits, and measurable acceptance gates. Avoid silently reducing an unqualified playback codec to a restricted keyframe/lossless/own-encoder-only demo. State justified scope assumptions and proceed.

Implement real algorithms, integrate through actual engine/container APIs, and verify independently:
- Decoder: independently produced streams and normative/reference output.
- Encoder: independent decoding, valid emitted bitstreams, useful quality, and actual rate behavior.
- Both: both independent paths, with self round trips as supplementary coverage.

Require applicable conformance, malformed-input/lifecycle/integration tests, recorded fuzz runs, release performance/resource measurements, and the project's CI checks. Keep unavailable/skipped/failed checks visible. Do not weaken tests, lints, thresholds, or required scope to claim completion.

Use “partial,” “unsupported,” “not run,” or “blocked” honestly. “Ship” requests completed implementation and validation; it does not by itself authorize publishing, merging, or releasing.

If the skill is missing, report the missing path, apply these core requirements, and continue non-blocked work. Do not claim the full skill was loaded.
